/*
 * Descriptions of the example programs for the API reference. Doxygen lists
 * each program with its source, linked to the documentation of the functions
 * it calls.
 */

/**
 * @page samples Examples
 * @details The programs show typical uses of the library, each with its source
 * and links to the functions it calls. The programs that use another library
 * are built with `SDFIO_BUILD_EXTERNAL_EXAMPLES` when that library is found.
 *
 * @section samples_c Examples in C
 * | Program | Shows |
 * | --- | --- |
 * | @ref writer.c | Writing a surface as ISO-2.0 with `float` heights and as ISO-1.0 with 16-bit integer heights, with a trailer. |
 * | @ref reader.c | Reading a file as `float` heights, printing the mean height and the trailer, and looking up a trailer field. |
 * | @ref streams.c | Reading a file from standard input one row at a time, without holding the matrix. |
 * | @ref excerpt.c | Saving the central part of a file as a new file with the header and the trailer of the file. |
 * | @ref coded_values.c | Writing and reading the 16-bit coded values of a scanner, including the points that are not measured. |
 * | @ref time_zone.c | Writing and reading the dates of an ISO-1.0 file through a time zone. |
 * | @ref allocators.c | Writing a file into RAM with all memory of the library from a static pool, for a device without a heap. |
 *
 * @section samples_external Examples With Other Libraries
 * | Program | Shows |
 * | --- | --- |
 * | @ref opencv.cpp | Reading a surface into an OpenCV image in place, smoothing it and writing it. |
 * | @ref eigen.cpp | Reading a surface into an Eigen matrix in place, removing the plane that fits it best and writing it. |
 * | @ref vtk.cpp | Reading a surface into VTK image data in place and showing it as a height field. |
 */

/**
 * @example writer.c
 * @details
 * Writes a surface as `float` values in ISO-2.0 and as 16-bit integer values in
 * ISO-1.0, each with the data type of the header set explicitly. The writer has
 * the time zone of the machine, which an ISO-1.0 file needs for its dates. The
 * trailer is written with sdfio_write_trailer_field().
 */

/**
 * @example reader.c
 * @details
 * Reads a file as heights in metres of type float, prints the revision, the
 * size and the mean height, and prints the trailer and then its fields one
 * after the other with sdfio_trailer_next_field(). It looks up the operator by
 * name with sdfio_trailer_find_field().
 */

/**
 * @example streams.c
 * @details
 * Reads a file from a stream, here standard input, one row at a time with
 * sdfio_read_data() and sdfio_decode_heights(), and prints the lowest and the
 * highest height. The program holds one row in memory, not the matrix.
 */

/**
 * @example allocators.c
 * @details
 * Writes a file into RAM, with all memory of the library from a static pool of
 * the program through an sdfio_allocator, as a scanner without a heap needs it.
 * The program sets a small buffer and a small chunk with
 * sdfio_writer_set_buffer() and sdfio_writer_set_chunk(), and the
 * file goes to a stream that writes into an array.
 */

/**
 * @example excerpt.c
 * @details
 * Saves the central part of a file as a new file. The header of the file is
 * copied with sdfio_header_copy() and gets the size of the part, and the
 * trailer is copied with sdfio_copy_trailer(), followed by a field of the
 * program. A layout with the row stride of the whole matrix writes the part in
 * place, without a copy of its heights.
 */

/**
 * @example coded_values.c
 * @details
 * Writes and reads the coded height information of a scanner as 16-bit values,
 * row by row, with sdfio_write_data() and sdfio_read_data(). The points that
 * are not measured carry the bad value SDFIO_BAD_INT16, which is recognized
 * when reading.
 */

/**
 * @example time_zone.c
 * @details
 * Writes and reads an ISO-1.0 file with an sdfio_time_zone of a fixed offset.
 * The file holds the clock of the writer, and the callbacks convert the creation
 * date between the clock and UTC with sdfio_date_to_time() and
 * sdfio_date_from_time(). sdfio_system_time_zone() is the zone of the machine. The program prints the date as written, as read with the time
 * zone, and as read without it, where the clock reading of the file is the
 * date.
 */

/**
 * @example opencv.cpp
 * @details
 * Reads the SDF file given as the first argument into an OpenCV image, smooths
 * it and writes it to the file given as the second, and reads the first file
 * into a region of a larger image. A negative row stride turns the surface into
 * an image without a copy.
 */

/**
 * @example eigen.cpp
 * @details
 * Reads the SDF file given as the first argument into an Eigen matrix, removes
 * the plane that fits its heights best, and writes the matrix to the file given
 * as the second. The strides of the layout are those of the matrix, so the
 * heights are read into it in place.
 */

/**
 * @example vtk.cpp
 * @details
 * Shows the SDF file given as the argument as VTK image data: the heights are
 * read in place into the array of the image, points that are not measured are
 * hidden, and the surface is displayed as a height field in a window.
 */
